{"t": 21.609, "n": 27, "ev": "result", "ok": true, "text": "Still on G5: I've read the specs and the check code, and I'm now ready to write up the finding.\n\nNeither spec says a kept row asks for no new opening. `_opened_rows` doesn't treat kept rows differently, and `_kept_marks` is only used for the mark check.\n\nARTIFACT gap_finding: Nothing states that a kept row asks for no new opening. `session-record-checks.spec.md` SRC-B5 (lines 46-54) has five scenarios and none mentions a mark, a kept row or an earlier visit. It fails any row whose existing file \"the session record shows no opening of\". SRC-AC7 (line 96) is the same. `kept-marks.spec.md` KMK-B1 to KMK-B9 (lines 9-65; criteria KMK-AC1 to AC17, lines 75-91) cover only accepting or refusing marks and the code step's rows. KMK-B1 defines a kept row as one with the same id, kind, requirement, file, proof and change as in its marking commit, and no later run commit changed its file. KMK-B8 covers only the code check's file diff. Searches for \"kept\", \"opened\" and \"open\" in both files found no link between the two. Code: `checks.py:203-224` (`_opened_rows`) builds `seen` from the one session's successful `read` calls (line 209) and fails every plan row with an existing file that isn't in `seen` (220-223). It looks at neither `row.completed` nor `_kept_marks`. `_kept_marks` (1046-1083) is the only code that decides a mark is kept, by marking commit, `fmt.row_text` equality and files touched since. It is called only from `_marks` (1090), with `ctx` and the plan path. `_opened_rows` is called at 1246 from the expand check. The kept rule can be reused: a row with `_kept_marks[row.id] == \"\"` is unchanged with a mark. Basis: read, not tested.", "subtype": "success", "is_error": false, "session_id": "1e7d345f-b1ca-4b23-91ee-6fb8a07e7657"}
